Section 42: On any roadway where traffic signs divide the roadway into two directions—one for upbound traffic and one for downbound traffic—with a median space or traffic markings indicating that the roadway is divided into two such directions, the driver shall drive on the left side of the roadway.

Section 43: A driver is prohibited from driving a vehicle:

(1) While impaired in the ability to drive;

(2) While intoxicated by alcohol or other intoxicants;

(3) In a manner that obstructs traffic;

(4) Recklessly or in a manner likely to cause alarm, which may endanger persons or property;

(5) In a manner abnormal to ordinary driving, or when unable to see the front or rear, on one side or both sides, sufficiently for safety;

(6) Straddling or crossing the lane dividing line or lane markings, except when changing lanes, turning, or making a U-turn;

(7) On the sidewalk without reasonable cause, except for baby strollers, or wheelchairs for the sick or disabled;

(8) Without regard for the safety or inconvenience of others;

(9) While using a mobile phone, except when using a hands-free accessory for conversation whereby the driver does not have to hold or grasp the mobile phone.

Section 43 bis: A driver is prohibited from consuming narcotic drugs under the law on narcotic drugs or consuming psychotropic substances under the law on psychotropic substances, as prescribed by the Commissioner-General of the Royal Thai Police by announcement published in the Government Gazette.

The chief traffic officer, inquiry official, traffic officer, or inspector shall have the authority to arrange for the testing of drivers of certain types of vehicles, as prescribed by the Commissioner-General of the Royal Thai Police by announcement published in the Government Gazette, to determine whether they have consumed narcotic drugs or psychotropic substances under paragraph one. If the preliminary test results show that the driver has not consumed such substances, the driver shall be allowed to continue driving.

In the event that a driver under paragraph two refuses to be tested, the chief traffic officer, inquiry official, traffic officer, or inspector shall have the authority to detain that person for the purpose of conducting the test for such period as is necessary under the circumstances, so that the testing may be completed promptly. Once that person consents to be tested and the preliminary test results show that they have not consumed such substances, they shall be released immediately.

Testing under this section shall be in accordance with the rules and procedures prescribed in the ministerial regulation.

Section 43 ter: In the event that there is reasonable cause to believe that any driver has violated Section 43 (1) or (2), the inspector shall have the authority to order that person to stop the vehicle and to order testing in accordance with Section 142 as well.
